    "turn_text": "My pleasure to have you here great radio stations across the land. I heart radio. The Blaze Radio Network. Three big hours coming your way of the weekend with Joe Pags. Alright, so we're gonna get to the iHeartRadio Music Festival because I wanna talk about it. We're gonna talk about that. We're gonna talk, we actually have an update in Gay News. Hold on. Gay News! There's always a gay news story that we have to get to here in the weekend for some reason. The strangest story you've heard yet, and what I think is happening is I think that people are trying to out-gay each other now. Really? Oh, you're gay? Well, watch this. I'm gay and I'm a woman. We've got that for you, which is always nice. uh... we've got a lot going on the president with an historic phone call to uh... the president of iran well brock obama chillin with the president of it of iran and i can't verify this but i'm pretty sure by the end of the phone call uh... obama was like uh... will you hang up and the guy from iran was like well not know you hang up first well no you hang up first so i think that actually well it might have happened at the end of the phone call what do you think they talked about And why is that even important today? I have an idea, no. I don't care about him talking to Iran. All of that and then some, including getting suspended from school for playing with a toy gun on your own property. But not before we get into what Ted Cruz did this week. You guys know who Senator Ted Cruz is? He's the junior senator from the Great State of Texas. He's a guy that pretty much came out of nowhere. He's a guy that almost didn't even win the nomination to be the Republican nominee in Texas for that open Senate seat. Now he almost lost to a more establishment guy, Lieutenant Governor David Dewhurst. You might not even have heard of Ted Cruz this week. Had it not been for a grassroots uprising to give people to know who he was in Texas. Now Ted Cruz is the biggest troublemaker in the Senate. His, Harry Reid, and Nancy Pelosi, and Barack Obama are scared to death of Ted Cruz. Now, what's interesting is, generally speaking, people in this country are trying to keep up with the Kardashians. Generally speaking, people in this country are liking and tweeting and Facebooking Lady Gaga. She's one of the largest Twitter followers on the Twitter. or Justin Timberlake. Or let me take another selfie and put it on Facebook and wonder why 14 people haven't liked it yet. I can't believe I posted that picture three minutes ago, nobody liked it. While all of that was going on, many of you heard the name Ted Cruz for the first time ever because A, the pop culture icons who are trying to keep you blind to what's going on in Washington or Sacramento or Austin or wherever you happen to live. Those people are scared to death of Ted Cruz and what he did this week. That's why you're hearing about him. NBC News called him dangerous. He's dangerous. ABC News, I believe, said that he was splitting the Republican Party or maybe political or somebody said that. People are freaking out over what he did. And what he did was he stood there for 21 hours and talked. What he did that's the scary thing Ted Cruz did but what he really did was he opened the eyes of the American people that in the United States Senate at least you don't just have to roll over and take it what he did was in the United States Senate he stood there for 21 hours got a little help from a couple of his friends Mike Lee and Rand Paul and Marco Rubio but generally speaking Ted Cruz spoke eloquently comprehensively understandably for 21 hours. And he talked about the problem with a law that he doesn't like. And it turns out it's a law that about 70% of us don't like Obamacare. Obamacare. The Affordable Care Act. That's the act where you can keep your plan that you have now. If you like it, not true. You'll pay the same thing as you're paying now. If you like your plan, that's not true. You can keep your doctor if you like him. That's not necessarily true. People who are full-time employees are seeing their hours knocked down to part-time so that the companies don't have to pay for health insurance as per this law. They're making it so difficult and so costly that these companies actually have to get away from paying that health insurance or paying partially for those benefits. Or else they'll go under. So the companies you're doing is they're cutting you down from full to part time. So they're not required by law to offer you benefits so that you can go to this government control government run government, um, planned health exchange, which is going to cost all of us more money. It's bad legislation. It's a bad law. I remember back in 2006, then president George W. Bush passed into law the border fence. It was interesting because, not that long before that, San Diego decided, you know what, we've got the largest border here with the largest numbers-wise border here with people coming across from Mexico illegally. We've got to do something about it, so they built a really, really good fence. All sorts of razor wire and it's double fence and it's really high, and they cut down on the border crossings in San Diego by 85%. So George W. Bush, not that he really wanted to because he wasn't great when it came to immigration. But because of the house in the Senate, he signed into law, I think they called it the secure offense act or something like that, where not only would you have a fence on the border between Mexico and the United States, you would have a double fence, make it really, really tough for people to break into this country illegally. He signed it into law. It was funded by the Congress. Let's go for it. In 2007, when the Democrats took over, what did they do? They didn't like the law. They disagreed with it. They hated it. So they defunded it. This defunding of Obamacare is nothing new. Shutting down the government is nothing new. The government was shut down during the Clinton administration. I think that probably gave him some extra time with the heavy said intern. But what I'm saying is they shut the government down in the Clinton administration to let him know you are not a dictator. You are not the ruler. You will have to compromise and deal with us or else there's going to be a problem. Well, now the threat of a government shutdown. It's really the court. The court where the ball is is really where the Democrats are. The House of Representatives said we're going to pass a spending bill, which funds the government pays for government services. We're going to give you that. We're going to pass it. But we're not going to fund Obamacare, something that the President is giving his friends exemptions from. All the members of Congress have exemptions from. We're not going to add the funding for Obamacare. We don't like the law. We think it's bad legislation. We're going to do what you guys did before. And we're going to defund something we don't like. But now you're hearing all the GOP hates the American people. Why do they want people to die? And the rhetoric is really intense and pretty horrible. But the fact is, it's not good legislation. The vast majority of the people who are represented by those in Congress hate it. And these guys are doing what they always thought they could do by saying, yeah, we're going to defund it. Yeah, I'm against Obamacare. Yeah, it's bad. And Ted Cruz said, here I am to save the day. And for 21 straight hours, he yapped. And then yapped some more. And then a little more yapping. He just went on and I like the energizer bunny of the legislative process. And what he did was he opened the American people's eyes to the fact that they're the bosses. They don't want this Obamacare. He's going to fight to try to stop it. He ran on that platform said that he would do it. And what we found out this week from Ted Cruz, the Senator, the Junior Senator from the great state of Texas, what we found was he just doesn't care. about the old garden washington i mean the one of his biggest critics was senator john mccain who claims to be a republican he's an embarrassment when it comes to to being a legislator he's somebody who should retire now and write a book about his his time that he served in the navy which was valiant he should now go and let that be his legacy because it's legacy in the senate is he's he's being an idiot who plays video poker on his iphone well important businesses getting done and then harry reed asks him to go out there and complain about ten crews and he does it like a doofus ten crews showed that he understands who works for he showed that he's not the ruling class he showed that he's subordinate to you in me that he's going to do everything he can anything in his powers as senator to do what we want him to do and he's going to continue to remind us i believe that the not only other people in charge but if the people don't don't put their feet down and say hey we are in charge and we lose freedom we lose liberty and we sit back and watch as the government takes over one-sixth of the economy with this obama care boondoggle that's what he did and as the media calls him dangerous and splitting the g o p and and uh... filled with rancor and and doesn't get it and do what does want to play ballman hates the people You and I will really know that Ted Cruz is on the right side of this. And the meeting's on the wrong side, and the John McCain's on the wrong side, and Harry Reid is on the wrong side, and Barack Obama, although he can sing some Al Green. He's on the wrong side of this one too. Your thoughts on what you saw out of the Senate this week. Your thoughts on Ted Cruz. I interviewed Cruz yesterday at length. It's a long interview. I might bring it to you at the bottom of the hour. I don't know if we can get the whole thing in though, because it's really long. We'll see. But I do want to hear from you. 1-800-501-7080. Back after this o